# 5️⃣ WHAT IS AN EIGENVECTOR?

This sounds scary initially.

Don't memorize the formula yet.

Think of a matrix as a transformation machine:

```text
Vector
  ↓
Matrix
  ↓
Transformed Vector
```

Usually the vector changes both:

```text
direction
magnitude
```

But some special vectors maintain their direction.

They may only be stretched or compressed.

Those are:

> **Eigenvectors**

---

# 6️⃣ EIGENVALUE

The amount by which the eigenvector is scaled is its:

> **Eigenvalue**

Mathematically:

$$
Av=\lambda v
$$

Where:

```text
A = matrix
v = eigenvector
λ = eigenvalue
```

Read it as:

> Matrix A transforms vector v, but the result is simply the same direction multiplied by λ.

---

# 🧠 ELI5

Imagine a rubber arrow.

A transformation stretches it.

Most arrows:

```text
↗
```

might rotate.

But one special arrow:

```text
→
```

might remain pointing in the same direction while becoming longer:

```text
→→→
```

That arrow is an eigenvector.

The stretch factor is the eigenvalue.

---

# 7️⃣ SIMPLE EXAMPLE

Consider:

$$
A=
\begin{bmatrix}
2&0\\
0&3
\end{bmatrix}
$$

Take:

$$
v=
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\
0
\end{bmatrix}
$$

Calculate:

$$
Av=
\begin{bmatrix}
2&0\\
0&3
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\
0
\end{bmatrix}
$$

Result:

$$
=
\begin{bmatrix}
2\\
0
\end{bmatrix}
$$

Which is:

$$
2
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\
0
\end{bmatrix}
$$

Therefore:

```text
Eigenvector = [1,0]
Eigenvalue = 2
```

---

# 8️⃣ ANOTHER EIGENVECTOR

Take:

$$
v=
\begin{bmatrix}
0\\
1
\end{bmatrix}
$$

Then:

$$
Av=
\begin{bmatrix}
0\\
3
\end{bmatrix}
$$

Therefore:

```text
Eigenvector = [0,1]
Eigenvalue = 3
```

So the matrix has two important directions:

```text
x direction → eigenvalue 2
y direction → eigenvalue 3
```

The second direction is stretched more.

---

# 9️⃣ WHY DOES THIS MATTER FOR PCA?

This is the important connection.

PCA uses eigenvectors of the **covariance matrix**.

The eigenvectors represent:

> Important directions in the data.

The eigenvalues represent:

> How much variance exists in those directions.

So:

```text
Covariance Matrix
       ↓
Eigenvectors
       ↓
Principal directions

Eigenvalues
       ↓
Amount of variance
```

🔥 This is the mathematical heart of PCA.

---

# 🔟 COVARIANCE MATRIX

Yesterday we learned covariance.

For multiple features, we can calculate covariance between every pair.

Suppose:

```text
X = [Age, Salary]
```

We get:

$$
Cov(X)=
\begin{bmatrix}
Var(Age) & Cov(Age,Salary)\\
Cov(Salary,Age) & Var(Salary)
\end{bmatrix}
$$

Example:

$$
\begin{bmatrix}
4&8\\
8&25
\end{bmatrix}
$$

Diagonal:

```text
4  → variance of Age
25 → variance of Salary
```

Off-diagonal:

```text
8 → covariance
```

