# 2️⃣4️⃣ INTERVIEW PREP 🎯

### Q1. What is PCA?

> PCA is a dimensionality-reduction technique that transforms correlated features into a smaller set of uncorrelated principal components while retaining as much variance as possible.

---

### Q2. What are eigenvectors in PCA?

> Eigenvectors of the covariance matrix represent the principal directions or axes of maximum variance in the data.

---

### Q3. What do eigenvalues represent?

> Eigenvalues indicate the amount of variance captured along their corresponding eigenvectors.

---

### Q4. Why standardize data before PCA?

> PCA is based on variance, so features with larger numerical scales can dominate the result. Standardization puts features on a comparable scale before calculating the covariance structure.

---

### Q5. PCA vs feature selection?

> Feature selection chooses a subset of the original features, whereas PCA creates new features called principal components from combinations of the original features.

